'uri': 'nyt://article/58afbaef-3977-55e9-af74-31344a1c0eff'}, {'abstract': 'For his final movie, Kiarostami added sound and movement to still photographs of lonely, beautiful scenes filled with snow, trees and animals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/01/movies/24-frames-review-abbas-kiarostami.html', 'snippet': 'For his final movie, Kiarostami added sound and movement to still photographs of lonely, beautiful scenes filled with snow, trees and animals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Images become cinema in “24 Frames,” the last movie from the Iranian filmmaker Abbas Kiarostami (1940-2016). “I always wonder to what extent the artist aims to depict the reality of a scene,” he reveals in the opening text. “Painters capture only one frame of reality and nothing before or after it.” This wondering led him to digitally transform 24 still images into short, visually and thematically linked sequences that make up this alternately charming and frustrating labor of love, which he worked on during the final three years of his life. (It was completed under the supervision of one of his sons.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/02/arts/02twentyfourframes/merlin_132839435_be8f90d1-6621-4c8e-a3ac-7ebe09f6a508-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/02/arts/02twentyfourframes/merlin_132839435_be8f90d1-6621-4c8e-a3ac-7ebe09f6a508-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/02/arts/02twentyfourframes/merlin_132839435_be8f90d1-6621-4c8e-a3ac-7ebe09f6a508-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 580, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'cre