 wild or politically pointed as the monster in Ahmed Saadawi’s “Frankenstein in Baghdad,” which leads our list of recommended titles this week. It’s not the only fiction with politics on its mind: You’ll find a pair of satirical Chinese novellas, a symphonic look at Germany’s fraught history and, in Leni Zumas’s “Red Clocks,” a dystopian vision of American patriarchy. In nonfiction, we have Thomas Jefferson’s daughters and Donald Trump’s erosion of democratic norms, along with the battle for civil rights, the experience of immigrant high schoolers, and memoirs about bird-watching and bipolar disorder. And yes, we know that Frankenstein was actually the name of the doctor.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/04/books/review/04edchoice-covers/04edchoice-covers-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/04/books/review/04edchoice-covers/04edchoice-covers-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/04/books/review/04edchoice-covers/04edchoice-covers-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 509, 'width': 1017, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/04/books/review/04edchoice-covers/04edchoice-covers-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 509, 'width': 1017, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/04/books/review/04edchoice-covers/04edchoice-covers-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'i