ate', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/5f78d16e-1b14-558b-9235-32f49aeb99ba', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/5f78d16e-1b14-558b-9235-32f49aeb99ba'}, {'abstract': 'This week we visited condos in the Entertainment District and in the Queen’s Quay area.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/01/realestate/homes-for-sale-in-toronto.html', 'snippet': 'This week we visited condos in the Entertainment District and in the Queen’s Quay area.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Toronto brokers predict the housing market may enter a slow patch after a new mortgage stress test came into effect at the start of the new year and Canada’s interest rates were increased in mid-January. Economists believe the interest rate could be raised twice more this year, potentially causing prospective buyers — especially first-time buyers — to rethink what kind of home they can afford and how soon they might move. Buyers at the high end are better positioned to weather tougher economic conditions, and luxury real estate will likely continue to see growth, brokers said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/realestate/11OTM-TORONTO-slide-NXCR/11OTM-TORONTO-slide-NXCR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/11/realestate/11OTM-TORONTO-slide-NXCR/11OTM-TORONTO-slide-NXCR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/realestate/11OTM-TORONTO-slide-NXCR/11OTM-TORONTO-slide-NXCR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/201