rganization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/30b28364-9ef7-5b1c-aa2e-57f707f6d408', 'word_count': 833, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30b28364-9ef7-5b1c-aa2e-57f707f6d408'}, {'abstract': 'A tenant in a co-op wants to see mold reports, but the board will not help.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/03/realestate/possible-mold-in-the-walls-and-an-unresponsive-board.html', 'snippet': 'A tenant in a co-op wants to see mold reports, but the board will not help.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q. I live in a co-op in Midtown West. My living room wall abuts the wall of a new building. My wall was damaged during its construction, and mold keeps growing on it. Because of a licensing agreement between my co-op board and the new development, the developer’s contractors have cleaned the mold and replaced the dry wall three times in two years. But, they do not share the mold test results with me, nor have they provided me with remediation paperwork; they say that verbal confirmation is enough. My co-op board and managing agent are not helping me get the paperwork either, ignoring my written requests. What can I do?', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/04/realestate/04ASK/04ASK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 117, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/04/realestate/04ASK/04ASK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 117}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/04/realestate/04ASK/04ASK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 200, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/04/realestate/04ASK/04ASK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 328, 'width': 1679, 'su