 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/03/pageoneplus/03a2_insider_superbowl3/03a2_insider_superbowl3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/02/03/pageoneplus/03a2_insider_superbowl3/03a2_insider_superbowl3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/03/pageoneplus/03a2_insider_superbowl3/03a2_insider_superbowl3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When They Zig, We Zag: How The New York Times Covers the Super Bowl', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Reporter’s Zigs and Zags  On the Sprint to the Super Bowl', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Super Bowl', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-02-03T16:54:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Insider', 'section_name': 'Times Insider', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Belson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f4dd8b9-45c9-5587-9240-116b81f81cac', 'word_count': 679, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f4dd8b9-45c9-5587-9240-116b81f81cac'}, {'abstract': 'When scientists recently discovered a protein that may help with the detection of sour tastes, they realized it had previously been identified in the inner ear.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/03/science/sour-tastes-ears.html', 'snippet': 'When scientists recently discovered a protein that may help with the detection of sour tastes, they realized it had previously been identified in the inner ear.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Of the five tastes — sweet, salty, sour, bitter and umami — sour is one of the most mysterious. Bite into a piece of lemon and — bing! — your brain gets a message that something sour has arrived. But unlike sweet and bitter, for example, for which biologists have identified proteins on the tongue’s taste cells that detect the molecules involved, the sourness of acids like lemon juice and vinegar has remained enigmatic, with the exact details of how we pick up on it little understood. Now, however, in a paper published last month in Science, researchers report that they have found a protein in mouse taste cells that is likely a key player in the detection of sour flavors.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/03/science/03TB-SOUR-alpha/03TB-SOUR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 414, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/03/science/03TB-SOUR-alpha/03TB-SOUR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 414}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/03/science/03TB-SOUR-alpha/03TB-SOUR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 707, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/03/science/03TB-SOUR-alpha/03TB-SOUR-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1414, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/03/science/03TB-SOUR-alpha/03TB-SOUR-thumbStandard.jpg',