: 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/04/us/savannah-talmadge-bridge-girl-scouts.html', 'snippet': 'After years of failed efforts to replace the name of Gov. Eugene Talmadge on a bridge in Savannah, Ga., the Girl Scouts have a plan to honor their founder.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAVANNAH, Ga. — The bridge that carries Highway 17 into Savannah is hard to miss: Its H-shaped towers are among the tallest structures for miles. But many people in the city would like never to lay eyes again on the green and white signs that say the span honors the segregationist former Gov. Eugene Talmadge.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/06/us/06savannah2/00savannah-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 436, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/06/us/06savannah2/00savannah-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 436}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/06/us/06savannah2/00savannah-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 743, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/06/us/06savannah2/00savannah-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1487, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/06/us/06savannah2/00savannah-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/02/06/us/06savannah2/00savannah-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/06/us/06savannah2/00savannah-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What It May Take to Strike a Segregationist’s Name From a Georgia Bridge: Hundreds of Girl Scouts', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Badge for the Girl Scouts: Scrubbing a Symbol of Segregation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Talmadge, Eugene', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bridges and Tunnels', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Girl Scouts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Names, Geographical', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Savannah (Ga)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Georgia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Low, Juliette Gordon', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monuments and Memorials (Structures)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stephens, Ron (1954- )', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Georgia Historical Society', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil Rights and Liberties', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-02-04T16:59:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Blinder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Blinder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad555720-e704-5e9e-b2c4-068762c30b4a', 'word_count': 1228, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad555720-e704-5e9e-b2c4-068762c30b4a'}, {'abstract': 'In “The World Only Spins Forward,” Isaac Butler and Dan Kois tell the story of Tony Kushner’s epic play in the words of the artists who made it and the fans who love it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/04/books/angels-in-america-oral-history-dan-kois-isaac-butler.html', 'snippet': 'In “The World Only Spins Forward,” Isaac Butler and Dan Kois tell the story of Tony Kushner’s epic play in the words of the artists who made it and the fans who love it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When “Angels in America” premiered on Broadway in 1993, The New York Times’s Frank Rich called it “the most thrilling American play in years.” Tony Kushner’s two-part epic about American life, set against the AIDS crisis and Ronald Reagan’s presidency, quickly became, by consensus, one of the 20th century’s most essential works of theater. (The play is coming back to New York, at the Neil Simon Theater, beginning previews this month and opening in March.) In 2016, Slate published an oral history of the show, in which Mr. Kushner and more than 50 others talked about the production’s long and often difficult road to success. Now the authors of that history — Isaac Butler, a theater director himself, and Dan Kois, an editor and writer for Slate — have published a new book that expands on it: “The World Only Spins Forward: The Ascent of ‘Angels in America.’” The book covers the development and life of the play, as well as Mike Nichols’s adaptation of it as a mini-series for HBO in 2003. Below, Butler and Kois talk about the influence of the political climate on their book, a film adaptation of “Angels” that never came to pass and more.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/05/books/05kois1/05kois1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 887, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/05/books/05kois1/05kois1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewi