}, {'firstname': 'Marcelo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rochabrún', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/756c4851-06be-589e-87d4-bfcdfce35173', 'word_count': 692, 'uri': 'nyt://article/756c4851-06be-589e-87d4-bfcdfce35173'}, {'abstract': 'At the heart of François Girard’s production of “Parsifal” is a 1,250-gallon pool of blood. Photos of a rehearsal, captured by The Times’s Damon Winter, reveal a world stained red.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/04/pageoneplus/parsifal-met-opera-blood-photos.html', 'snippet': 'At the heart of François Girard’s production of “Parsifal” is a 1,250-gallon pool of blood. Photos of a rehearsal, captured by The Times’s Damon Winter, reveal a world stained red.', 'lead_paragraph': 'François Girard’s production of Richard Wagner’s “Parsifal,” opening at the Metropolitan Opera on Monday, is an affair drenched in blood. It stains every aspect of the show, a meditation on the knights of the Holy Grail, the vessel that supposedly caught Jesus’ blood as he hung from the cross. It also floods the Met’s vast stage: 1,250 gallons of fake blood “made from a recipe that includes tap water, glycerin, and red and blue dye, mixed to taste,” writes Michael Cooper, who attended a rehearsal. Damon Winter, a Times photographer, joined Mr. Cooper, capturing scenes of a world stained red: the feet of singers and dancers, their costumes, the set. His photos drip with the stuff. Mr. Winter recently explained how he got the shot.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/05/pageoneplus/05insider/05insider-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/05/pageoneplus/05insider/05insider-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/05/pageoneplus/05insider/05insider-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/05/pageoneplus/05insider/05insider-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/05/pageoneplus/05insider/05insider-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/02/05/pageoneplus/05insider/05insider-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/05/pageoneplus/05insider/05insider-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Take Pictures at a Blood Bath', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Best Angle for a Blood Bath', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Opera', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wagner, Richard', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Parsifal (Opera)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-02-05T01:37:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Insider', 'section_name': 'Times Insider', 'byline': {'original': 'By Raillan Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Raillan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2df1a2ec-47ff-5e94-aff2-3badb88602cf', 'word_count': 607, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2df1a2ec-47ff-5e94-aff2-3badb88602cf'}, {'abstract': 'Is it too much to ask New York’s warring mayor and governor to work together in the face of a near-existential crisis?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/04/opinion/cuomo-deblasio-subway-chaos.html', 'snippet': 'Is it too much to ask New York’s warring mayor and governor to work together in the face of a near-existential crisis?', 'lead_paragraph': 'After imperial Japan swept into China in the 1930s, the warring Chinese Communists and Nationalists managed a semblance of unity against a common enemy, even if it took some arm twisting. When Hitler’s Germany imperiled Europe, the United States and the Soviet Union set aside mutual hostility to defeat the Nazis.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/05/opinion/05mon1/05mon1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/05/opinion/05mon1/05mon1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/05/opinion/05mon1/05mon1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/05/opinion/05mon1/05mon1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1199, 'width': 1199, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': No