: 'persons', 'value': 'Lieberman, Hallie', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Vibrator Nation: How Feminist Sex-Toy Stores Changed the Business of Pleasure (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Comella, Lynn', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-02-06T10:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peggy Orenstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peggy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Orenstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/38ff4417-b33d-52de-a388-ab470e4f67d9', 'word_count': 1347, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38ff4417-b33d-52de-a388-ab470e4f67d9'}, {'abstract': 'A selection of books published this week; plus, a peek at what our colleagues around the newsroom are reading.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/06/books/review/new-noteworthy-matthew-schneier.html', 'snippet': 'A selection of books published this week; plus, a peek at what our colleagues around the newsroom are reading.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HOW TO BREAK UP WITH YOUR PHONE By Catherine Price. (Ten Speed, paper, $12.99.) We’re all addicted. That’s not big news. But are there practical ways to unplug and, as Price puts it, “take back your life”? She has a plan, a 30-day plan. EVERYTHING HAPPENS FOR A REASON By Kate Bowler. (Random House, $26.) Bowler, a professor at Duke Divinity School, had a perspective-altering experience at 35 when she learned she had late-stage colon cancer. This is a memoir about her disillusionment with the “prosperity gospel,” that American belief that to good people come only good things. She doesn’t think this anymore. BEING WAGNER By Simon Callow. (Vintage, paper, $16.95.) Author of a monumental biography of Orson Welles, Callow now turns to an equally operatic subject: Richard Wagner, his life and times. BUILDING THE GREAT SOCIETY By Joshua Zeitz. (Viking, $30.) The inner workings of the White House, with its war room intensity, never ceases to capture readers’ attention. Zeitz delves here into Lyndon B. Johnson’s administration, capturing both the atmosphere and the advisers (Bill Moyers and Jack Valenti, among others) who made Johnson’s vision a reality. A LITERARY TOUR DE FRANCE By Robert Darnton. (Oxford, $34.95.) Darnton continues his decades-long exploration of how the publishing industry worked in France on the eve of the revolution. Using a trove of documents from a Swiss publisher that smuggled illegal works over the border, he is able to piece together a complex network that put subversive books in the hands of French men and women.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/books/review/11newnoteworthy1/11newnoteworthy1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 296, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/11/books/review/11newnoteworthy1/11newnoteworthy1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 296}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/books/review/11newnoteworthy1/11newnoteworthy1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 911, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/books/review/11newnoteworthy1/11newnoteworthy1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 911, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/books/review/11newnoteworthy1/11newnoteworthy1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/02/11/books/review/11newnoteworthy1/11newnoteworthy1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/books/review/11newnoteworthy1/11newnoteworthy1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New & Noteworthy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New &amp; Noteworthy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-02-06T10:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5dfa15fc-8cad-5920-8855-b6745a7524b5', 'word_count': 443, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5dfa15fc-8cad-5920-8855-b6745a7524b5'}, {'abstract': 'Nothing reveals the curious disposability of consumer technology more than this feeble, aging device.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/06/magazine/what-i-learned-from-watching-my-ipads-slow-death.html', 'snippet': 'Nothing reveals the curious disposability of consumer technology more than this feeble, aging device.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My old iPad just turned five, and it’s starting to die. If it could wonder about such things, it might question this prognosis. Its memory, after all, still retrieves information as quickly as it ever did. Its face hasn’t aged a day, projecting as vividly as it did in 2012, when Apple called it “stunning” and “gorgeous.” It hasn’t suffered vision loss; the camera still works. The touch-screen works. Buttons work. Speaker, headphone jack, charging port: All still do what I ask of them. On examination, almost nothing about the device seems to have changed. And yet it’s starting to give up, and so am I.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/magazine/11mag-ontech1/11mag-ontech1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 698, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/11/magazine/11mag-ontech1/11mag-ontech1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 698}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/magazine/11mag-ontech1/11mag-ontech1-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 881, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/magazine/11mag-ontech1/11mag-ontech1-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1761, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/magazine/11mag-ontech1/11mag-ontech1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/02/11/magazine/11mag-ontech1/11mag-ontech1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/magazine/11mag-ontech1/11mag-ontech1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What I Learned from Watching My iPad’s Slow Death', 'kicker': 'On Technology', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPad', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-02-06T10:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Herrman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Herrman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/625ee515-ff75-559a-bcf3-f2af8d86aeae', 'word_count': 1378, 'uri': 'nyt://article/625ee515-ff75-559a-bcf3-f2af8d86aeae'}, {'abstract': 'In “Fire Sermon,” the author of the story collection “I Want to Show You More” describes a married woman’s love affair.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/06/books/review/fire-sermon-jamie-quatro.html', 'snippet': 'In “Fire Sermon,” the author of the story collection “I Want to Show You More” describes a married woman’s love affair.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FIRE SERMON By Jamie Quatro 208 pp. Grove Press. $24', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/books/review/11Gaige/11Gaige-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/11/books/review/11Gaige/11Gaige-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/books/review/11Gaige/11Gaige-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/books/review/11Gaige/11Gaige-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/books/review/11Gaige/11Gaige-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/02/11/books/review/11Gaige/11Gaige-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/books/review/11Gaige/11Gaige-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jamie Quatro’s First Novel Centers on Religious and Sexual Passion', 'kicker': 'Fiction', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The End of the Affair', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Quatro, Jamie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Fire Sermon: A Novel (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-02-06T10:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amity Gaige', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amity', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gaige', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f89f031f-f792-503a-a7e4-bc155421a818', 'word_count': 1198, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f89f031f-f792-503a-a7e4-bc155421a818'}, {'abstract': 'Even in its new incarnation as a boast, it can’t shed its dehumanizing past.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/06/magazine/the-cost-of-being-savage-in-a-supposedly-civilized-world.html', 'snippet': 'Even in its new incarnation as a boast, it can’t shed its dehumanizing past.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This country is rich with awful things to say about everybody. There’s a slur for you and a slur for me — more than one. And because we’re terrified of dealing with them head on, we’ve made them just as easy to warp and defang. Ask anybody who has played HQ Trivia. HQ is the 12-question smartphone fad that has turned Americans into daily-quiz junkies. And in any given session, a stumper might come along that wipes out a huge number of players. (On Nov. 14, nearly half the players misidentified the proper spelling of “embarrassed.”) These apocalyptic decimations so impress HQ’s host, Scott Rogowsky, that he practically bursts with glee: “Savage question!” he’ll exclaim.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/magazine/11mag-firstwords-copy/11mag-firstwords-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/11/magazine/11mag-firstwords-copy/11mag-firstwords-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/magazine/11mag-firstwords-copy/11mag-firstwords-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/magazine/11mag-firstwords-copy/11mag-firstwords-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1400, 'width': 1399, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/magazine/11mag-firstwords-copy/11mag-firstwords-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75,