person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liptak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d062b150-7d36-5fe2-88cf-d7a35890747f', 'word_count': 460, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d062b150-7d36-5fe2-88cf-d7a35890747f'}, {'abstract': 'As front offices increasingly rely on analytics and statistical models, big contracts for aging free agents are becoming less attractive.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/06/sports/baseball/free-agents.html', 'snippet': 'As front offices increasingly rely on analytics and statistical models, big contracts for aging free agents are becoming less attractive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The paradox of baseball economics has been obvious for years. In general, older players make more money, but younger players are better. The system rewards more experienced players for years of weathering baseball’s relentless demands, enduring the grind to reach the open market for a well-earned windfall.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/07/sports/07kepnerweb-1/merlin_133376135_7fcdc9d4-1b63-43f5-8623-f45876f8e13b-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/07/sports/07kepnerweb-1/merlin_133376135_7fcdc9d4-1b63-43f5-8623-f45876f8e13b-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/07/sports/07kepnerweb-1/merlin_133376135_7fcdc9d4-1b63-43f5-8623-f45876f8e13b-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 675, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'imag