gion since the 17th century. The cookies, once meant to dip in Champagne (which wasn’t dry then) and now often served as a snack with coffee and tea, are sweet but not exceedingly so, and only lightly flavored with vanilla. They are pink like bubble gum (they’re tinted with carmine, a red food coloring) and shaped like rectangles (they’re baked in special molds). But their most distinctive characteristic is their extreme crispness — no matter how dainty you aim to be, each bite produces crumbs, a small flurry of powdered sugar and a crackle that can be heard across the table.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/magazine/11mag-ondessert-promo/11mag-11ondessert-t_CA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/11/magazine/11mag-ondessert-promo/11mag-11ondessert-t_CA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/magazine/11mag-ondessert-promo/11mag-11ondessert-t_CA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/magazine/11mag-ondessert-promo/11mag-11ondessert-t_CA1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/magazine/11mag-ondessert-promo/11mag-ondessert-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/02/11/magazine/11mag-ondessert-promo/11mag-ondessert-thumbStanda