0', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicole Perlroth', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicole', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perlroth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f45d854b-4585-5761-afc0-bbaf922a738e', 'word_count': 1245, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f45d854b-4585-5761-afc0-bbaf922a738e'}, {'abstract': 'How the “Drugs” graphic featured in last night’s show came to be — with a special T twist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/08/t-magazine/raf-simons-drug-sweatshirt-cookie-mueller.html', 'snippet': 'How the “Drugs” graphic featured in last night’s show came to be — with a special T twist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the early ’80s, the embodiments of downtown N.Y.C. culture Cookie Mueller and Glenn O’Brien co-wrote the play “Drugs,” a tragicomedy about two roommates and life-altering substances through the decades. It never made it to the stage (other than a one-time-only public reading at La Mama), becoming part of that era’s blurry subcultural lore, although its existence was mentioned in the 2014 Mueller biography “Edgewise.” T’s senior art director and a co-founder of the Kingsboro Press, Daniel Wagner, took note. “In the grand scheme of things, Cookie doesn’t have a lot of longer works published,” he says. Along with Seth Zucker of For the Common Good (a like-minded independent publishing practice) and Kingsboro’s other co-founder, Megan Plunkett, he tracked down the manuscript. “We thought it would be exciting to read, since she’s such a evocative writer, and exciting to resurface, because she’s such a beloved underground figure,” Wagner says.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/08/t-magazine/fashion/09tmag-drugs-01/09tmag-drugs-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/08/t-magazine/fashion/09tmag-drugs-01/09tmag-drugs-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/08/t-magazine/fashion/09tmag-drugs-01/09tmag-drugs-01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/08/t-magazine/fashion/09tmag-drugs-01/09tmag-drugs-01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/08/t-magazine/fashion/09tmag-drugs-01/09tmag-drugs-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/02/08/t-magazine/fashion/09tmag-drugs-01/09tmag-drugs-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/08/t-magazine/fashion/09tmag-drugs-01/09tmag-drugs-01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From a Cult Biography to the Raf Simons Runway', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Simons, Raf', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-02-08T20:05:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hilary Moss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hilary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/875d7a12-a6a7-5727-b7d0-358200f64c07', 'word_count': 378, 'uri': 'nyt://article/875d7a12-a6a7-5727-b7d0-358200f64c07'}, {'abstract': 'Suggested reading from critics and editors at The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/08/books/review/12-new-books-we-recommend-this-week.html', 'snippet': 'Suggested reading from critics and editors at The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sometimes a book grabs you because it’s especially topical, or relevant to your life. Other times the appeal is more idiosyncratic: a strong voice, a great story, a brilliant mind at play. This week’s recommended titles offer distinctive and wide-ranging pleasures — beginning with a couple of distinctive and wide-ranging essay collections from Martin Amis and Zadie Smith, celebrated novelists who also rank among the best literary critics of their generations. Read them, and you’ll come up with your own recommendations. Or read more of ours! There’s a harrowing autobiographical novel about birth and death all at once, along with memoirs from a Chinese filmmaker and an indigenous Canadian writer. (I told you the list was wide-ranging.) For good measure we also offer a couple of World War II thrillers, one fiction and one non, and deep dives into French food, Israeli politics and the roots of environmentalism.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/11/books/review/11edchoice1/11edchoice1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 304, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/11/books/review/11edchoice1/11edchoice1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xla