eir fortunes rose and fell with geysers of oil.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/11/books/bryan-mealer-kings-of-big-spring.html', 'snippet': 'In “The Kings of Big Spring,” Bryan Mealer writes about four generations of his family, and how their fortunes rose and fell with geysers of oil.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a 27-year-old with three children, Bobby Mealer got a phone call from his friend Grady Cunningham, who asked: “How’d you like to be a millionaire?” In that place and time, Texas in 1981, this was not an idle question. Mealer was living in Alvin, outside of Houston, working at a chemical plant. Cunningham was in Big Spring, a West Texas town on the precipice of an oil boom; not its first. Cunningham had started his own drilling company, and wanted his friend to be his vice president.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/12/books/12mealer1/12mealer1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 892, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/12/books/12mealer1/12mealer1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 892}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/12/books/12mealer1/12mealer1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 689, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/12/books/12mealer1/12mealer1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1378, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/12/books/12mealer1/12mealer1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStanda