_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benedict Carey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benedict', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa648298-83fd-5007-87c2-0507092de5bc', 'word_count': 978, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa648298-83fd-5007-87c2-0507092de5bc'}, {'abstract': 'Porter Road in Tennessee ships fresh pork chops, steaks and poultry nationwide.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/12/dining/porter-road-butcher-nashville.html', 'snippet': 'Porter Road in Tennessee ships fresh pork chops, steaks and poultry nationwide.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A new website from Porter Road, a chef-owned butcher shop in Nashville, sells meat from small farms, delivered fresh, nationally and within two days. James Peisker and Chris Carter opened their store in 2011 after having difficulty finding meat that satisfied them for their fledgling catering company. The meats and poultry are pasture-raised by farmers who do not use antibiotics or hormones on the animals. The Porter Road partners do their own butchering, dry-age meats for 14 days, and offer a range of cuts, though some are limited and wait-listed. Prices and shipping charges are moderate. My Kansas City bone-in strip loin, a little more than a pound, was $21, for example: Porter Road, porterroad.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/14/dining/14burnermeat/merlin_133069115_1a565fce-68bb-4120-b463-f2740201fdbc-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/14/dining/14burnermeat/merlin_133069115_1a565fce-68bb-4120-b463-f2740201fdbc-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/14/dining/14burnermeat/merlin_133069115_1a565fce-68bb-4120-b463-f2740201fdbc-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/14/dining/14burnermeat/merlin_133069115_1a565fce-68bb-4120-b463-f2740201fdbc-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/14/dining/14burnermeat/14burnermeat-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/02/14/dining/14burnermeat/14burnermeat-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/14/dining/14burnermeat/14burnermeat-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Meats and Poultry Just a Click Away', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Cook: Meat and Poultry  Just a Click Away', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Porter Road Butcher (Nashville, Tenn)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Meat', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poultry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nashville (Tenn)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Butchers and Butchering', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-02-12T17:42:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa8dd63a-0e98-555f-817f-09a7ad509020', 'word_count': 138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa8dd63a-0e98-555f-817f-09a7ad509020'}, {'abstract': 'Gunnar Gislason, the executive chef of Agern, developed the ninth flavor from Icelandic Provisions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/12/dining/skyr-icelandic-provisions.html', 'snippet': 'Gunnar Gislason, the executive chef of Agern, developed the ninth flavor from Icelandic Provisions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the past two years, Icelandic Provisions has been making skyr, a thick, custardy fermented milk similar to yogurt, in upstate New York. For its ninth flavor, the company asked the chef Gunnar Gislason, who has been advising the company, to devise a flavor. Mr. Gislason, who owns a restaurant in Reykjavik and is also the executive chef of Agern in Grand Central Terminal, dipped into his childhood and the Icelandic countryside for an elegant mauve-colored cherry-black currant skyr. Icelandic Provisions’s skyr, which is made with cultures from Iceland, is creamier and not as tart as what he grew up eating, he said. “Skyr like this is an amazing development,” he said: Skyr Cherry-Black Currant, $1.75 to $1.99 for 5.3 ounces, icelandicprovisions.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/14/dining/14burnerskyr/merlin_132729471_82dbbed5-a4df-4ced-959f-0824b106e15c-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 408, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/14/dining/14burnerskyr/merlin_132729471_82dbbed5-a4df-4ced-959f-0824b106e15c-articleLarg