st trying patients.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Doctors and patients are supposed to share a therapeutic alliance. This agreement hinges on trust and respect. But what happens when that trust breaks down because of addiction? In a recent On Medicine column, Dr. Siddhartha Mukherjee writes about a patient he treated early in his career, whose deceptions to obtain opioids pulled him into a patient-doctor relationship that he eventually describes as “mutual assured destruction.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/09/magazine/09mag-opioids1/09mag-opioids1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/09/magazine/09mag-opioids1/09mag-opioids1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/09/magazine/09mag-opioids1/09mag-opioids1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/09/magazine/09mag-opioids1/09mag-opioids1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/09/magazine/09mag-opioids1/09mag-opioids1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/02/09/magazine/09mag-opioids1/09mag-opioids1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/09/magazine/09mag-opioids1/09mag-opioids1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Health Care Professionals Share Their Stories About Opioid-Addicted Patients', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2018-02-13T10:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alice Yin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alice', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b28bccda-8d45-5760-8fec-7635becf7c7f', 'word_count': 1370, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b28bccda-8d45-5760-8fec-7635becf7c7f'}, {'abstract': 'The Berlin Wall has been down as long as it stood. One generation lived with the wall, one without it. But in many ways it is still a tale of two countries.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/13/world/europe/berlin-wall-equinox-east-germany.html', 'snippet': 'The Berlin Wall has been down as long as it stood. One generation lived with the wall, one without it. But in many ways it is still a tale of two countries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERLIN — Durs Grünbein was conceived two months after the Berlin Wall was built, in the cold winter of 1961. He spent half his life behind the wall, or, as he prefers to put it: “I spent one life as a hostage and another life free.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/12/world/12Wall1/merlin_99872800_1694b203-e9e6-4320-8272-073f768d2159-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/12/world/12Wall1/merlin_99872800_1694b203-e9e6-4320-8272-073f768d2159-articleLa