ns', 'value': 'Chinatown (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hwa Yuan Szechuan (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-02-13T17:24:37+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Food', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/93810cad-2f23-5599-a41d-10e957fafdf8', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/93810cad-2f23-5599-a41d-10e957fafdf8'}, {'abstract': 'The director Ali Soozandeh uses rotoscoping, a form of animation, in this ensemble piece involving a prostitute, a judge and a musician, among others.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/13/movies/tehran-taboo-review.html', 'snippet': 'The director Ali Soozandeh uses rotoscoping, a form of animation, in this ensemble piece involving a prostitute, a judge and a musician, among others.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Tehran Taboo” gains much of its power from its use of rotoscoping, a form of animation (familiar to viewers of “Waking Life” and “Tower”) in which the drawing is done over filmed actors. What is provocative about the technique here is that it’s a sort of workaround — a way for the director, Ali Soozandeh, who grew up in Iran and now lives in Germany, to tell a story that he says would have been impossible to film in Tehran.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/14/movies/14tehrantaboo/merlin_133544495_b8490e57-e47c-4fbd-8fc5-009919f88ae3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 251, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/14/movies/14tehrantaboo/merlin_133544495_b8490e57-e47c-4fbd-8fc5-009919f88ae3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlarg