iddlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stanford', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/c92a24af-c3d6-5df1-9d77-d38dc0454e7e', 'word_count': 1179, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c92a24af-c3d6-5df1-9d77-d38dc0454e7e'}, {'abstract': 'In year two, the Trump administration drifts back toward its campaign-season promises.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/14/opinion/trump-populism-republican-party.html', 'snippet': 'In year two, the Trump administration drifts back toward its campaign-season promises.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the striking features of Donald Trump’s first year in office was the all-but-complete abandonment of the populist economic vision upon which he successfully campaigned. Candidate Trump stiff-armed Republican economic orthodoxy and won the Midwest by promising a flood of infrastructure spending, universal health care, protectionism, middle-class tax cuts — a right-wing Keynesianism for the common man. But as president he defaulted to Paul Ryan’s agenda, chasing the Tea Party dream of Obamacare repeal, issuing a budget proposal thick with discretionary spending cuts, and pursuing a tax reform tilted heavily toward corporations.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/14/opinion/14Douthat-web/merlin_133456341_c5f227c1-9cdf-4f1b-8b82-fe1e9ee0827d-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/14/opinion/14Douthat-web/merlin_133456341_c5f227c1-9cdf-4f1b-8b82-fe1e9ee0827d-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/14/opinion/14Douthat-web/merlin_133456341_c5f227c1-9cdf-4f1b-8b82-fe1e9ee0827d-jumbo.jpg', 'height':