role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d311f2e-7a68-558f-93f2-51db32e4683d', 'word_count': 1119, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d311f2e-7a68-558f-93f2-51db32e4683d'}, {'abstract': 'Following the artist’s surrealist candy-colored video for the St. Vincent single “New York,” the creative pair debut a second neon-tinted collaboration at Karma Gallery.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/16/t-magazine/alex-da-corte-st-vincent.html', 'snippet': 'Following the artist’s surrealist candy-colored video for the St. Vincent single “New York,” the creative pair debut a second neon-tinted collaboration at Karma Gallery.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I’ve been a St. Vincent fan for a long time, and more recently a very big Annie fan,” says the Philadelphia-based artist Alex Da Corte of Annie Clark, the real-world alter-ego of the guitar-shredding rock act. They met last summer when she invited him to direct the music video to her single “New York,” a melancholic love song at odds with the cheerfully absurdist visuals he paired it with — a Manhattan ultra-saturated with effervescent colors, one in which Clark spins the iconic Astor Place cube round and round with a pair of fuschia-clad legs dangling out of it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/16/t-magazine/art/16tmag-dacorte-slide-4C3E/16tmag-dacorte-slide-4C3E-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/16/t-magazine/art/16tmag-dacorte-slide-4C3E/16tmag-dacorte-slide-4C3E-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/16/t-magazine/art/16tmag-dacorte-slide-4C3E/16tmag-dacorte-slide-4C3E-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1