nion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Gibson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gibson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/fdaab6c4-6a07-5eee-9c34-e69454c83e37', 'word_count': 1205, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fdaab6c4-6a07-5eee-9c34-e69454c83e37'}, {'abstract': 'A restriction compatible with a vision of armed citizenship.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/17/opinion/sunday/no-country-for-young-men-with-ar-15s.html', 'snippet': 'A restriction compatible with a vision of armed citizenship.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Four years ago the essayist Helen Andrews wrote a critique, for the religious journal First Things, of what she described as “bloodless moralism” — meaning the decay of public moral arguments into a kind of a vulgar empiricism, a mode of debate so cringingly utilitarian that it can’t advance the most basic ethical claim (“Do not steal …”) without a regression analysis to back it up (“… because bicycle thieves were 4 percent less likely to obtain gainful employment within two years of swiping their neighbor’s Schwinn”).', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/18/opinion/sunday/18douthat-print/18douthat-print-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/18/opinion/sunday/18douthat-print/18douthat-print-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/18/opinion/sunday/18douthat-print/18douthat-print-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 676, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'r