print_headline': 'Counting On That ‘Harry Potter’ Magic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Harry Potter and the Cursed Child (Play)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ambassador Theater Group', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lyric Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thorne, Jack (1978- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tiffany, John (1971- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Friedman, Sonia', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Callender, Colin', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-02-21T10:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roslyn Sulcas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roslyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sulcas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0198ab95-2a97-5cd6-9b5a-235fc7c6bb31', 'word_count': 2032, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0198ab95-2a97-5cd6-9b5a-235fc7c6bb31'}, {'abstract': 'Monsters, golems and doppelgängers range through these sublime new collections of short fiction.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/21/books/review/new-science-fiction-fantasy.html', 'snippet': 'Monsters, golems and doppelgängers range through these sublime new collections of short fiction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than any other genres, I think, science fiction and fantasy are in constant conversation with their past, present and future. Entering those worlds as a child meant growing into an awareness of how deeply they depend on a kind of exchange between readers and writers — almost inevitably, new works of science fiction and fantasy inherit a sense of engagement, whether it shows up as homage, critique or collaboration. Three recent books of short fiction, each of them fantastically hybrid, join the conversation and demonstrate just how overt its dynamics can be.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/25/books/review/25Otherworldly/25Otherworldly-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 522, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/25/books/review/25Otherworldly/25Otherworldly-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 522}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/25/books/review/25Otherworldly/25Otherworldly-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 891, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/25/books/review/25Otherworldly/25Otherworldly-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1782, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/25/books/review/25Otherworldly/25Otherworldly-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/02/25/books/review/25Otherworldly/25Otherworldly-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/25/books/review/25Otherworldly/25Otherworldly-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Latest in Science Fiction and Fantasy', 'kicker': 'Otherworldly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Science Fiction and Fantasy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science Fiction', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Monster Portraits (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Samatar, Del', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Samatar, Sofia (1971- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Weight of Words (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McKean, Dave', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Starlings (Book)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walton, Jo', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-02-21T10:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amal El-Mohtar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amal', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'El-Mohtar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/20636f7f-5b27-5833-a38e-295324fd8d3c', 'word_count': 1415, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20636f7f-5b27-5833-a38e-295324fd8d3c'}, {'abstract': 'New research suggests that for the economy to become more productive, demand matters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/21/upshot/the-economy-is-getting-hotter-is-a-productivity-boom-next.html', 'snippet': 'New research suggests that for the economy to become more productive, demand matters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two of the most important facts about the global economy over the last decade are these: A giant financial crisis led to mass unemployment in many countries and years of disappointing growth. And despite a seeming barrage of technological innovation, productivity growth has been the weakes