, 'value': 'American Civil Liberties Union', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-02-21T10:00:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Washington', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ron Nixon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nixon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/579991ac-fccf-5b15-a89e-63dbc202f6cc', 'word_count': 1410, 'uri': 'nyt://article/579991ac-fccf-5b15-a89e-63dbc202f6cc'}, {'abstract': 'Infinitely less popular than “Spring,” but not without its charms.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/21/magazine/letter-of-recommendation-vivaldis-winter.html', 'snippet': 'Infinitely less popular than “Spring,” but not without its charms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Antonio Vivaldi’s “Spring” has more than twice as many film credits as any of its counterparts in his Baroque masterpiece, “The Four Seasons.” “Spring” is weddings and day spas, your toddler’s I.Q.-inflating sleep aid. “Spring” is the sassy classical of the baby boomers, music to accompany an anniversary toast at an expensive French restaurant. But, as my fellow millennials know far too well, an ill-timed frost nips yesteryear’s aspirations in the bud. Now is the time for “Winter,” a composition that, like the season, holds us in a tighter grip.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/25/magazine/25mag-lor/25mag-25lor-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 437, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/25/magazine/25mag-lor/25mag-25lor-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 437}