niversity of Michigan', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-02-22T10:45:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bret Stephens', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bret', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stephens', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/140d98a5-848d-5e39-968b-1fb9aacb88c0', 'word_count': 3412, 'uri': 'nyt://article/140d98a5-848d-5e39-968b-1fb9aacb88c0'}, {'abstract': 'Five of the city’s emerging musical talents in spring’s crop of retro-inspired Hawaiian shirts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/22/t-magazine/japan-musicians-yurufuwa-gang-bo-ningen.html', 'snippet': 'Five of the city’s emerging musical talents in spring’s crop of retro-inspired Hawaiian shirts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nene (left) and Ryugo Ishida (right), shown here in the residential district of Maruyama-cho, first met at a nightclub in 2016. Both solo musicians at the time, they quickly realized they shared a love of rap and tattoos. (The two are very well inked.) Their friendship inevitably led to the recording studio, where they made their first album as Yurufuwa Gang, “Mars Ice House,” which came out last April. Just as their look borrows from many sources — including ’50s Americana and ’80s-era hip-hop culture — their music is equally uncategorizable, moving from candyish pop to ’90s slow jams. “We don’t think a lot about what other artists are doing; we’re focused on different things,” Ishida says. The same is true when they tattoo each other. As Nene says, “We ink whatever we feel inspired to — the same way we make music.”', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '59', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/12/t-magazine/12tmag-aloha-slide-XV7V/12tmag-aloha-slide-X