y', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael M. Grynbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/53ff758e-8ce5-5410-b64d-8838aa9d9822', 'word_count': 565, 'uri': 'nyt://article/53ff758e-8ce5-5410-b64d-8838aa9d9822'}, {'abstract': 'The red-breasted merganser is a sleek, speedy seabird with a decidedly punk hairdo.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/22/nyregion/goth-of-the-bay.html', 'snippet': 'The red-breasted merganser is a sleek, speedy seabird with a decidedly punk hairdo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I remember spotting my first red-breasted merganser in Brooklyn as it paddled along the bulkheads lining Jamaica Bay. It was a strangely warm February day, snow lingered amid the shoreline grasses and the bird peeked in and out of a drifting, intermittent fog. I was an excited young bird-watcher, and any bird was worth looking up in one of the three field guides I carried with me.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/21/nyregion/00NATURE/merlin_10710690_aa7c9667-5056-44a0-9b77-eb259ded5a1d-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 429, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/21/nyregion/00NATURE/merlin_10710690_aa7c9667-5056-44a0-9b77-eb259ded5a1d-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 429}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/21/nyregion/00NATURE/merlin_10710690_aa7c9667-5056-44a0-9b77-eb259ded5a1d-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 510, 'width': 714, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None