: 1}, {'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eligon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9441598-64e2-5fc0-bfc6-8cef8c9e30bc', 'word_count': 1222, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9441598-64e2-5fc0-bfc6-8cef8c9e30bc'}, {'abstract': 'Suggested reading from critics and editors at The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/22/books/review/10-new-books-we-recommend-this-week.html', 'snippet': 'Suggested reading from critics and editors at The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, books offer highly detailed accounts of love, politics and war around the world. In “The Heart Is a Shifting Sea,” Elizabeth Flock provides an unusually intimate look at three married couples in Mumbai. Marci Shore’s latest is about the bravery and ideals of Ukrainians who faced beatings and death during political demonstrations in 2013. And Steve Coll’s “Directorate S,” a sequel to his Pulitzer Prize-winning “Ghost Wars,” examines the United States’ entanglement in Afghanistan and Pakistan. Also this week: a selection of the best stories by postmodernist pioneer Robert Coover; a remarkably readable and respectful book about the abortion debate; a novel about a 440-year-old man suffering a midlife crisis; a history of how Christianity became the dominant religion of the Roman Empire; a new private eye introduced by Walter Mosley; and two standout works of historical fiction.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/25/books/review/25ed-covers/25ed-covers-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 295, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/25/books/review/25ed-covers/25ed-covers-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight'