a Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Landler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Landler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c59cff81-0388-599e-a184-4da30b9011f7', 'word_count': 1335, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c59cff81-0388-599e-a184-4da30b9011f7'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. McAdams, of “Spotlight,” “The Notebook” and “Mean Girls,” talks about her competitive side and returning to a comedic role in “Game Night.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/23/movies/rachel-mcadams-game-night.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. McAdams, of “Spotlight,” “The Notebook” and “Mean Girls,” talks about her competitive side and returning to a comedic role in “Game Night.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before there was Rachel McAdams, the Academy Award nominee and tough-as-nails star of films like “Spotlight” and television series like “True Detective,” and before there was Rachel McAdams, the romance queen and star of “The Notebook” and “The Time Traveler’s Wife,” there was Rachel McAdams, who gained wide attention for her go-for-broke performances in comedies like “The Hot Chick” (playing a body-swapped Rob Schneider) and “Mean Girls” (as the ruthless high-school queen bee Regina George).', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/25/arts/25SNAPSHOT/25SNAPSHOT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 910, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/25/arts/25SNAPSHOT/25SNAPSHOT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 910}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/25/arts/25SNAPSHOT/25SNAPSHOT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 676, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/25/arts/25SNAPSHOT/25SNAPSHOT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1351, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/25/arts/25SNAPSHOT/25SNAPSHOT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/02/25/arts/25SNAPSHOT/25SNAPSHOT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/25/arts/25SNAPSHOT/25SNAPSHOT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No Matter What Rachel McAdams Plays, She Plays for Keeps', 'kicker': 'A Word With', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rachel McAdams Plays for Keeps', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McAdams, Rachel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Game Night (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-02-23T15:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/36a38e11-3320-539f-a04d-f7316ce450b4', 'word_count': 1083, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36a38e11-3320-539f-a04d-f7316ce450b4'}, {'abstract': '“Lady Bird” shares many similarities with “Real Women Have Curves.” But there are more systemic barriers to blame for the film’s dismissal by Oscar voters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/23/movies/lady-bird-real-women-have-curves.html', 'snippet': '“Lady Bird” shares many similarities with “Real Women Have Curves.” But there are more systemic barriers to blame for the film’s dismissal by Oscar voters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you recognize where a story is headed, you usually feel disappointed or comforted by the familiar. The tropes and stock characters have robbed the story of its ability to surprise you. That’s always a threat with a genre so well worn as the coming-of-age film — the probably nostalgic tale of a child or teenager inching toward adulthood. Yet entries like Greta Gerwig’s “Lady Bird” still prove that this type of movie has the ability to move us.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/25/arts/25LADY-BIRD-REAL-WOMEN1/25LADY-BIRD-REAL-WOMEN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 405, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/25/arts/25LADY-BIRD-REAL-WOMEN1/25LADY-BIRD-REAL-WOMEN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 405}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/25/arts/25LADY-BIRD-REAL-WOMEN1/25LADY-BIRD-REAL-WOMEN1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 691, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/25/arts/25LADY-BIRD-REAL-WOMEN1/25LADY-BIRD-REAL-WOMEN1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1381, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 