ction_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Perlez and Javier C. Hernández', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perlez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Javier', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Hernández', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/9bd9efa9-58ac-5dd8-a3b8-8dba91390b88', 'word_count': 1226, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9bd9efa9-58ac-5dd8-a3b8-8dba91390b88'}, {'abstract': 'Progressives don’t vote as often as conservatives do, which makes turnout the biggest opportunity for the new progressive movement.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/25/opinion/democrats-voter-turnout.html', 'snippet': 'Progressives don’t vote as often as conservatives do, which makes turnout the biggest opportunity for the new progressive movement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Trump presidency has brought a political awakening for American progressives. It began even before he took office, with the organizing for the Women’s March. Then came the citizen activists who protested at airports and later helped save health insurance for millions of people. Now high school students are trying to transform the gun debate.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/26/opinion/26leonhardtWEB/merlin_89074888_71ef3da7-5341-4fa7-9221-6ebe6229a280-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/26/opinion/26leonhardtWEB/merlin_89074888_71ef3da7-5341-4fa7-9221-6ebe6229a280-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url'