also contending with the timeless.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every year, when the Nobel Prize in Literature is awarded, there’s usually an article or two published about which past recipients’ works have failed to stand the test of time — say, those of Pearl S. Buck, the first American woman to win the award, whose 1930s novels about Chinese peasant life now read as twee and patronizing Orientalist fairy tales.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '40', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/13/t-magazine/13tmag-chart/13tmag-chart-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 243, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/13/t-magazine/13tmag-chart/13tmag-chart-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 243}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/13/t-magazine/13tmag-chart/13tmag-chart-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 414, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/13/t-magazine/13tmag-chart/13tmag-chart-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 829, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/13/t-magazine/13tmag-chart/13tmag-chart-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/02/13/t-magazine/13tmag-chart/13tmag-chart-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/13/t-magazine/13tmag-chart/13tmag-chart-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'th