': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Schwirtz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwirtz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/47336626-4aee-51aa-b004-e0011f4faf53', 'word_count': 1523, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47336626-4aee-51aa-b004-e0011f4faf53'}, {'abstract': 'You can be entitled to your opinion, compensation, a fair trial — or you could be a rude, entitled person. It all depends what we think you deserve.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/27/magazine/how-much-is-anyone-entitled-to-in-the-end.html', 'snippet': 'You can be entitled to your opinion, compensation, a fair trial — or you could be a rude, entitled person. It all depends what we think you deserve.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I was in my 20s, I worked at a video-game company in Silicon Valley. We were expected to work 12-hour days and at least one weekend day per week. The pay was terrible, as was the product. Even so, it was part of the job to feel lucky to have it, and, being young and replaceable, we were in no position to complain. To complain would have made us “entitled,” which we were not entitled to be. The company, on the other hand, was entitled to claim our every waking hour, and to treat its rush to meet deadlines for clunky, slow-moving shooter games as a collective labor of love. It felt difficult to square the democratic world outside the office with the feudal arrangement inside it; I spent a lot of time marveling at the cognitive dissonance of it all.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/04/magazine/04mag-firstwords/04mag-04firstwords-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 580, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/03/04/magazine/04mag-firstwords/04mag-04firstwords-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 580}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/04/magazine/04mag-firstwords/04mag-04firstwords-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 990, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/04/magazine/04mag-firstwords/04mag-04firstwords-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1751, 'width': 1811, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/04/magazine/04mag-firstwords/04mag-04firstwords-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/03/04/magazine/04mag-firstwords/04mag-04firstwords-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/04/magazine/04mag-firstwords/04mag-04firstwords-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Much Is Anyone ‘Entitled’ To, in the End?', 'kicker': 'First Words', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Uncivil Rights', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Millennial Generation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customs, Etiquette and Manners', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lasch, Christopher', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date'