'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e40eef1-ea39-52a5-a5af-bf7c29c4c52c', 'word_count': 1277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e40eef1-ea39-52a5-a5af-bf7c29c4c52c'}, {'abstract': 'In “Blue Dreams,” the psychologist Lauren Slater explores the intersection of personality and chemistry by way of her own history with antidepressants.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/27/books/review-blue-dreams-mental-illness-drugs-lauren-slater.html', 'snippet': 'In “Blue Dreams,” the psychologist Lauren Slater explores the intersection of personality and chemistry by way of her own history with antidepressants.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The world’s first transorbital lobotomy was performed in 1946 by Walter Freeman, in his Washington office. Using an ice pick from his own kitchen, he went through the eye sockets into the brain of his patient, a 29-year-old severely depressed housewife, and cut into her frontal lobes. Then he sent her home in a cab.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/28/arts/28BOOKSLATER1/28BOOKSLATER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 874, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/28/arts/28BOOKSLATER1/28BOOKSLATER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 874}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/28/arts/28BOOKSLATER1/28BOOKSLATER1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 703, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/28/arts/28BOOKSLATER1/28BOOKSLATER1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 938, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'ca