 arms control.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'National Identity Is Made Up', 'kicker': 'The Interpreter: Episode 2', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'vis-multimedia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-02-28T10:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Video', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad1f80e9-6e4b-5f32-b82e-1946c862bf0f', 'word_count': 52, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad1f80e9-6e4b-5f32-b82e-1946c862bf0f'}, {'abstract': 'Jane Kamensky published a biography of John Singleton Copley. Then she got a tattoo of his art.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/28/books/review/jane-kamensky-tattoo-john-singleton-copley.html', 'snippet': 'Jane Kamensky published a biography of John Singleton Copley. Then she got a tattoo of his art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is a truth too seldom acknowledged that books mark their authors for life. Authors, of course, mark the completion of their books: a party, a bauble, for the lucky few, a check. Yet the wages of a book go deeper than royalties or celebration. So it was that on a Saturday morning last year, just shy of my 54th birthday, I found myself at a trendy tattoo parlor on the Lower East Side, waiting for my artist to arrive. The plan: She would ink onto my forearm a detail from a painting by the Boston-born artist John Singleton Copley, whose biography I had recently written. I spent the better part of a decade in the close company of that fascinating, vexing man, dead some 200 years yet far more alive to me than most of my neighbors. Now he and I were both moving on. A remembrance seemed in order. “A life should leave / deep tracks,” as the poet Kay Ryan writes.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/04/arts/04KAMENSKY-01/merlin_134263352_29dee6e3-2d60-4b1b-802a-c8c408d71ae3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/03/04/arts/04KAMENSKY-01/merlin_134263352_29dee6e3-2d60-4b1b-802a-c8c408d71ae3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/04/arts/04KAMENSKY-01/merlin_134263352_29dee6e3-2d60-4b1b-802a-c8c408d71ae3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/04/arts/04KAMENSKY-01/merlin_134263352_29dee6e3-2d60-4b1b-802a-c8c408d71ae3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1067, 'width': 1600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/04/arts/04KAMENSKY-01/04KAMENSKY-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/03/04/arts/04KAMENSKY-01/04KAMENSKY-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/04/arts/04KAMENSKY-01/04KAMENSKY-01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Writing a Book Leaves a (Literal) Mark on Its Author', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bookmarked', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'A Revolution in Color: The World of John Singleton Copley (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Copley, John Singleton', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tattoos', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-02-28T10:00:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Kamensky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kamensky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe975748-410d-5b40-9d82-1ed31457a05e', 'word_count': 988, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe975748-410d-5b40-9d82-1ed31457a05e'}, {'abstract': 'It’s less accurate than Fahrenheit, but that’s the key to its charm.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/28/magazine/letter-of-recommendation-celsius.html', 'snippet': 'It’s less accurate than Fahrenheit, but that’s the key to its charm.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A little over a decade ago, I moved to Montreal expecting, perhaps foolishly, that I would become fluent in French. It was a goal that proved elusive — I just couldn’t master the Quebecois accent, which was almost inscrutably nasal compared with the Parisian French I learned in high school. I stuttered through one quotidian exchange after another, painfully aware I was marking myself as a clumsy outsider. It was exhausting. Somewhat chastened, I gave up on French and turned my attention to a new language: Celsius.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'ra