s.com/2018/02/28/movies/werewolf-review.html', 'snippet': 'The austere truths of Ashley McKenzie’s drama are bracing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A tone of stark austerity seems to have become the default mode of contemporary films about drug addiction (Josh and Bennie Safdie’s excellent 2015 film “Heaven Knows What” being a notable exception). This holds for “Werewolf,” a Canadian film written and directed by Ashley McKenzie. But the particulars of Ms. McKenzie’s approach, and the excellent work of her lead actors, bring new value to the mode.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/27/arts/werewolf1/merlin_134247764_8036bc8a-9c58-4a38-916f-d2f59f55ba16-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/27/arts/werewolf1/merlin_134247764_8036bc8a-9c58-4a38-916f-d2f59f55ba16-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/27/arts/werewolf1/merlin_134247764_8036bc8a-9c58-4a38-916f-d2f59f55ba16-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/27/arts/werewolf1/merlin_134247764_8036bc8a-9c58-4a38-916f-d2f59f55ba16-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/27/arts/werewolf1/werewolf1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/02/27/arts/werewolf1/werewolf1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75