f practical nylon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tie-dye has long been associated with hippies and counterculture. And lately, fashion designers seemed to have caught a whiff. In January, the British designer Alex Mullins featured the psychedelic print on boxy suits and long T-shirts. The buzzed-about Russian designer Gosha Rubchinskiy created an oversize tie-dyed T-shirt that features an image of an alien playing with a soccer ball. And the young and obscure street wear brand Cactus Plant Flea Market sells vintage-style T-shirts that are hand-printed and embroidered with offbeat typography and graphics. To stoke demand, the shirts are available only at surprise drops at stores like Dover Street Market. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/01/fashion/02PULSE4/02PULSE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/03/01/fashion/02PULSE4/02PULSE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/01/fashion/02PULSE4/02PULSE4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/01/fashion/02PULSE4/02PULSE4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/01/fashion/02PULSE4/02PULSE4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/03/01/fashion/02PULSE4/02PULSE4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/01/fashion/02PULSE4/02PULSE4-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New for Spring: Tie-Dyes, Hawaiian Shirts and Nylon Bags', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Calvin Klein Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Louis Vuitton', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-02-28T18:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Men’s Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alex Tudela', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alex', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tudela', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e05b0dc-1a2a-540f-948e-ed4955dfc223', 'word_count': 497, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e05b0dc-1a2a-540f-948e-ed4955dfc223'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Glass, Carnegie Hall’s composer in residence this season, had two orchestral works featured on a concert by the Louisiana Philharmonic Orchestra.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/28/arts/music/review-louisiana-philharmonic-carnegie-hall.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Glass, Carnegie Hall’s composer in residence this season, had two orchestral works featured on a concert by the Louisiana Philharmonic Orchestra.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the last decade, Carnegie Hall’s composer in residence position has tended to go to veteran iconoclasts whose innovations have taken root in the broader classical scene. Meredith Monk, Louis Andriessen and Kaija Saariaho, for example, have all been celebrated with the concert series that come with the Carnegie post, the Debs Composer’s Chair.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/01/arts/01lpo/merlin_134709660_549d91a0-72eb-4ad4-8a30-468fc2c2900b-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/03/01/arts/01lpo/merlin_134709660_549d91a0-72eb-4ad4-8a30-468fc2c2900b-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/01/arts/01lpo/merlin_134709660_549d91a0-72eb-4ad4-8a30-468fc2c2900b-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/01/arts/01lpo/merlin_134709660_549d91a0-72eb-4ad4-8a30-468fc2c2900b-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/01/arts/01lpo/01lpo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/03/01/arts/01lpo/01lpo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/01/arts/01lpo/01lpo-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Philip Glass Is Celebrated, but Not With His Best', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Philip Glass Celebration', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Louisiana Philharmonic Orchestra', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Glass, Philip', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-02-28T18:00:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Seth Colter Walls', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Seth', 'middlename': 'Colter', 'lastname': 'Walls', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/96ad59ca-9f17-5d3f-9dc7-27ec2a63031b', 'word_count': 543, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96ad59ca-9f17-5d3f-9dc7-27ec2a63031b'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. McGregor’s piece, different each time it’s performed, has impressive surface style and dancers. But they can’t mask its lack of shape or heart.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/28/arts/dance/review-company-wayne-mcgregor-autobiography.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. McGregor’s piece, different each time it’s performed, has impressive surface style and dancers. But they can’t mask its lack of shape or heart.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even with a title as seemingly straightforward as “Autobiography,” a dance by the brainy British choreographer Wayne McGregor isn’t likely to be plain memoir. And sure enough, the work, which had its United States debut at the Joyce Theater on Tuesday, was partly generated through science. Mr. McGregor had his genome sequenced. Before each performance a computer algorithm based on that biographical data determines the sequencing of the dance’s 23 sections. So each performance is different.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/03/01/arts/01mcgregor-2/01mcgregor-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'wi